WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.139 --> 00:00:02.789
In 1881 an architect called William Brown

00:00:02.789 --> 00:00:05.219
and an iron foundry owner
called Benjamin Haugh

00:00:05.219 --> 00:00:07.576
patented an idea for a new type of jail.

00:00:07.576 --> 00:00:09.360
The Rotary Jail.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.491
In hindsight, it was not a
good idea

00:00:11.491 --> 00:00:14.029
It's impractical, unsafe,
and massively overcomplicated.

00:00:14.029 --> 00:00:15.849
But that didn't stop them selling the idea

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:18.699
to a few towns across the United States.

00:00:18.699 --> 00:00:21.969
And the first one was built here,
in Crawfordsville, Indiana.

00:00:21.969 --> 00:00:23.840
And while it hasn't held
prisoners for decades,

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.171
it is the only one where
the mechanism still works.

00:00:27.171 --> 00:00:29.019
We have a two story rotary jail,

00:00:29.019 --> 00:00:31.499
and there are eight cells on each floor.

00:00:31.499 --> 00:00:34.379
It weighs approximately
30 tonnes, it's made of steel.

00:00:34.379 --> 00:00:37.190
It's operated by a hand crank,
the hand crank moves both

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:39.137
floors of the jail at
exactly the same time.

00:00:39.137 --> 00:00:42.486
It doesn't touch any of the
walls, it doesn't touch the bars

00:00:42.486 --> 00:00:46.116
the whole machine
sits around a central cylinder

00:00:46.116 --> 00:00:49.206
that runs from the
roof to the sewer system.

00:00:49.206 --> 00:00:52.476
And it works like a pin,
all of the weight of it rests

00:00:52.476 --> 00:00:54.857
on the lip on top of that pin.

00:00:54.857 --> 00:00:57.926
It's just a marvel of
engineering for its time.

00:00:57.926 --> 00:01:01.206
The advantage of
having a rotational cell block

00:01:01.206 --> 00:01:03.348
was that you could have
just the one opening,

00:01:03.348 --> 00:01:05.546
the inmates could
enter/exit their cells from

00:01:05.546 --> 00:01:08.546
in front of a pie shaped cell block.

00:01:09.467 --> 00:01:12.337
So if you only had one door
to watch, then you didn't have

00:01:12.337 --> 00:01:16.126
to pay as many guards to be
here to watch the inmates.

00:01:16.126 --> 00:01:17.580
There's just one way out.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:21.980
The half-moon seat shapes are toilets,
they've been welded shut.

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:26.380
So when our jail was built
in the 1880s our inmates had

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:29.700
indoor plumbing and toilets
inside their cells when

00:01:29.708 --> 00:01:32.186
most of the country didn't
have it in their homes.

00:01:32.186 --> 00:01:36.106
But some of the issues with
the jail are that from 1882

00:01:36.106 --> 00:01:40.340
until 1938 there was just
one opening on each floor.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:42.920
And there's an independently
operating sliding door

00:01:42.926 --> 00:01:45.436
that locks closed on each
floor to keep inmates just from

00:01:45.436 --> 00:01:47.076
jumping out whenever they want to.

00:01:47.076 --> 00:01:50.474
If there was a fire, you
risked having to have a guard

00:01:50.474 --> 00:01:53.097
stand there and crank
out each cell one by one

00:01:53.097 --> 00:01:54.777
to get the inmates to safety.

00:01:54.777 --> 00:01:58.156
The other issue is that
almost 50% of our inmates were

00:01:58.156 --> 00:02:00.897
arrested for being drunk,
some of them would over spill

00:02:00.897 --> 00:02:03.546
from the drunk tank and
need to be in the cells

00:02:03.546 --> 00:02:06.266
to make room for other
inmates as they came in.

00:02:06.266 --> 00:02:08.874
So they would fall asleep on
the ground, hugging the bars

00:02:08.874 --> 00:02:10.787
and the machine would begin to move,

00:02:10.787 --> 00:02:13.446
and essentially the wall
would come into contact

00:02:13.446 --> 00:02:16.206
with their limbs and break
them or amputate them.

00:02:16.206 --> 00:02:20.300
They quit rotating the jail in 1938,

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.900
they cut an individual door
out of every cell

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:24.900
and they installed the catwalk.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.276
So they quit rotating it because
the problems that they had

00:02:27.276 --> 00:02:29.584
with fire concerns and with inmates

00:02:29.584 --> 00:02:31.513
getting limbs chopped off.

00:02:31.513 --> 00:02:34.676
As far as we've been told, as
long as we keep maintenancing

00:02:34.676 --> 00:02:36.507
the machine the way that
we do and treating it

00:02:36.507 --> 00:02:38.907
the way that we do,
there's no end in sight.

00:02:38.907 --> 00:02:40.800
We'll be able to turn it forever.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:42.446
There are any number of ways
that this could have been improved.

00:02:42.446 --> 00:02:45.020
The bars could have spun,
not the cells.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:47.580
or you could remove the
complicated, expensive rotation

00:02:47.586 --> 00:02:50.627
part entirely, and just, you
know, open one cell at a time.

00:02:50.627 --> 00:02:52.876
Which is basically a regular jail.

00:02:52.876 --> 00:02:54.515
The moral of the story?

00:02:54.515 --> 00:02:56.606
Just because an idea has been patented

00:02:56.606 --> 00:02:59.346
doesn't mean that it's actually any good.

00:02:59.346 --> 00:03:00.953
Just means no one's thought of it before

00:03:00.953 --> 00:03:04.286
and sometimes there's a reason for that.

